course: dictionary
lesson: 2755
title: Disaster: big trouble and total failure
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A disaster is a terrible event that causes a lot of damage, suffering or death, like a flood, an earthquake or a big crash. It can also be something that goes very badly or fails completely. Read the words around it to see which meaning fits.
explain: «Early programs were not typed on screens: people punched holes into cards... dropping the stack in the wrong order was a disaster.» Nobody was hurt here. The work was ruined, so disaster means something went very badly.
explain: Disaster is a noun, and its plural is disasters. People also use it as a joke: a burnt cake can be «a disaster». Ask yourself: is this serious harm, or just a total failure? That tells you which meaning fits.
fact: A disaster is a terrible event, or something that goes very badly or fails completely.
fact: Natural disasters include floods, earthquakes, wildfires, hurricanes and tsunamis.
fact: Families can make emergency plans, so everyone knows what to do if a natural disaster happens.
fact: Useful phrases: «a total disaster», «end in disaster», «disaster area».
fact: People sometimes call small mistakes «a disaster» as a joke, but real disasters cause serious harm.
exercise: «Live shows often have small disasters, so teams keep safety pins, tape and spare costumes ready.» What does disaster mean here? | A famous singer | A surprise party | A huge earthquake | *Something that goes wrong, like a costume falling apart
exercise: What is a natural disaster? | *A terrible event, like a flood, earthquake or wildfire | A kind of tasty cake | A quiet day at school | A type of musical instrument
exercise: What is the plural of disaster? | disasterses | *disasters | disastres | disasterous
test: «A slippery slope says one small step will lead to disaster.» What does disaster mean here? | A happy ending | A flood or an earthquake | *Something going very badly | A holiday by the sea
test: Which phrase means something failed completely? | A quiet afternoon | A bright new idea | *A total disaster | A gentle breeze
exam: Dad told the story of his spelling-bee «disaster», and they laughed together. What does disaster mean here? | A happy party | *Something that went very badly for him | A prize he won | A very big flood
